    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A growth in demand for online and independent learning has led to the rise of adoption of new technologies such as artificial intelligence (AI) virtual assistants. While these AI teaching assistants have been prevalent in a number of educational spaces across the globe, little is known about how they affect disability education. Thus, the present study investigated a study including blind and non blind teachers’ perceptions about the impact and implementation of AI teaching assistants in the classroom, particularly focusing on their use for students with blindness/ visual impairment (B/VI). Through qualitative teacher interviews and classroom observations at California’s School for the Blind, the study captures perceptions from various types of teacher profiles, highlighting the nuanced advantages and challenges associated with AI virtual assistants. The findings indicate that key themes to merging AI-based teaching with blind/low vision (BLV) education are (1) enhancing accessibility and personalization, (2) engagement of the students, (3) technical integration, and (4) evolving teacher/student experiences. Data from the research also underscores the critical role of AI virtual assistants in bridging the accessibility needs of visually impaired students, such as the need for independent learning and social inclusion, or providing detailed feedback to compensate for visual limitations. It also highlights the importance of extensive teacher training that goes beyond basic tech usage due to the various types of blind/non blind teachers, focusing on methods for incorporating AI to foster dynamic conditions in the classroom. These findings indicate a substantial gap in aligning the duality of advancing access to inclusive technology with robust teacher training strategies, emphasizing the need for improved integration to effectively support visually impaired students.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
